course: reflection
lesson: 2
title: Grief has no timetable: houses, anniversaries and big questions
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Grief has no timetable: it does not stop on a set day, and it can be stirred up again by a house, a date, a song or a question in the dark.
explain: When Lily's grandma died, the family sold her house, and every time they drove past Lily thought, "Strangers are gonna live in Grandma's kitchen. It's like she's being wiped out"; for months she would not go down that street.
explain: Then she found an old recipe in Grandma's handwriting, and cooking it with Mum every Sunday made her feel close to Grandma again: places hold memories, but memories live in people, not only in bricks and mortar.
explain: Loss also brings big questions, like "Where is he now?" or "Will Mum and Dad die too?"; families believe different things, nobody can know for sure, and asking a trusted adult out loud usually makes the scary thought quieter than keeping it alone at night.
explain: And not every loss is a death: a parent going to prison or a friend far away can hurt in the same way, and that hurt is real too.
fact: Places such as a grandparent's house hold memories, so losing them can feel like another loss; "bricks and mortar" means just a building.
fact: You can be sad about someone you never met, such as a baby cousin who died before or at birth, especially when people you love are sad; there is no rule about who is allowed to grieve.
fact: When a young person dies, the world can suddenly feel unsafe; the fear is usually big at first and shrinks with time, and most children grow up safe.
fact: Watching news reports about a death again and again tends to keep the fear fresh rather than calm it.
fact: People believe different things about what happens after death, such as heaven or an afterlife or nothing at all; nobody can know for sure, and each family has its own way of thinking about it.
fact: People grieve in different ways; some need normal things like playing and laughing to cope, and laughing does not mean someone is not sad.
fact: A new pet does not replace one that died; you can love both, and waiting until you feel ready is okay.
fact: Many children start thinking about death around nine or ten; it can be scary, talking about it helps, and most people live a very long time.
fact: "Existential" questions are big questions about life and why we are here; almost everyone wonders about them sometimes.
fact: Losing someone can happen in other ways than death, such as a parent going to prison ("doing time"); a parent's mistake is not the child's mistake.
fact: You can love someone and also know they did something wrong.
fact: An anniversary is the date something happened, years later; a sad date and a happy date such as a birthday can be the same day, and people often find ways to hold both, like lighting a candle.
fact: We cannot keep everyone in our minds all the time; forgetting someone for a while is not the same as not caring.
fact: Writing a letter you can never send can still help the feelings, and saying thank you to people while you can is a good habit, not a debt.
exercise: Lily won't drive down Grandma's old street after the house is sold. Why does it hurt so much? | The house holds memories, so losing it feels like another loss | Houses keep a person alive, so selling it makes her die all over again | She wanted to live in the house herself and is jealous | Old houses are always sad places for children to see
exercise: What helped Lily feel close to her grandma again? | Cooking Grandma's handwritten recipe with Mum every Sunday | Refusing to talk about the house ever again at home with anyone | Knocking on the new owners' door to ask for the house back for good | Making the family promise never to sell anything of Grandma's again
exercise: Oscar's baby cousin died before being born. Oscar never met him but feels weird and sad. Is he allowed to grieve? | Yes; there is no rule about who is allowed to be sad | No; you can only grieve for people you knew well | No; only the baby's parents are allowed to feel sad | Yes, but only once he is a grown-up himself
exercise: Kai's ten-year-old teammate died in a car crash. Kai thinks, "If it can happen to him, it can happen to me." What is true? | The fear is big at first and shrinks; most children grow up safe | Children are now in more danger every day than grown-ups are | He will feel exactly this scared for the rest of his life, every day | Thinking about it will make the same thing happen to him
exercise: What did NOT help Kai with his fear? | Watching the news reports about the crash again and again | The memorial with the whole football team together | Talking to the school counsellor about the crash | Playing a match in his teammate's honour
exercise: Isla's mum says Grandad is in heaven; her friend says people just stop. Who is right? | Nobody can know for sure; families believe different things | Isla's mum is right, and her friend is lying to her on purpose | Her friend is right, and her mum is lying to her | Whoever is older is always right about such things
exercise: What helped Isla with the heaven question? | Asking several people and hearing different answers kindly | Arguing with her friend until one of them gave in and said sorry | Never asking anyone at all, so she would not upset anyone at home | Deciding that whoever shouts the loudest about it must be right
exercise: Sam laughs and plays at school a week after his dad died. Ben wonders if Sam even loved his dad. What is true? | Some people need normal things like playing to cope | Laughing proves Sam is not sad about his dad at all | Sam has already finished grieving for good | Ben should ask him straight out why he isn't sad
exercise: Amelia's parents offer a new rabbit after Snowy dies. Why does she say no at first? | A new pet would not replace Snowy, and she isn't ready yet | Rabbits are dangerous pets after the first one dies | She has decided never to love any animal again | Her parents only want a new rabbit so that she will forget Snowy
exercise: Noah hears that Great-Uncle Bill died and starts thinking, "Everyone dies. What's the point?" What is true? | Many children start wondering about death around his age, and talking helps | Only children with something wrong with them think about death | He should keep these thoughts secret so nobody worries | Thinking about death means he will die soon himself
exercise: What did Noah's dad say that helped? | That he'd be around a long, long time, and it's okay to wonder | That Noah must stop thinking about it immediately | That nobody ever dies if they eat up all their vegetables every day | That it is rude to ask questions about death
exercise: Chloe's dad went to prison and she wonders, "Am I bad?" What is true? | A parent's mistake is not the child's mistake | Children always share the blame for what parents do | She must stop loving her dad to show she is good | Kids whose dads are in prison will end up there too
exercise: Aaron's grandma died on his birthday. What did he slowly decide to do? | Light a candle for her each birthday and still celebrate | Never celebrate his birthday again for the rest of his life | Move his birthday to a different day of the year | Pretend that his grandma had died on another date
exercise: Ella hears that an old teacher who moved to Spain has died, and she had not thought of her in a year. What is true? | Forgetting for a while is not the same as not caring | Ella should feel guilty because she forgot to think of her | It is too late to do anything at all with her feelings | Only people we think about every day are worth missing
exercise: What helped Ella with the thanks she never said? | Writing a thank-you letter, even though it could not be sent | Telling herself the teacher never deserved any thanks from anyone | Blaming herself until the guilt went away by itself | Promising never to like a teacher again
test: Your nan from Yorkshire says, "Don't be mardy, our Lily, it's only bricks and mortar." ("Mardy" means sulky.) What is the kindest true answer? | It's a building, but it holds memories, so missing it is real | Nan is right, houses are only bricks and can never matter to anyone | Lily should stay away from the street forever | Lily should ask the new owners to give the house back
test: What would happen if Oscar kept all his questions about his baby cousin to himself? | He would likely stay confused and scared on his own | He would understand everything better without asking | His sadness would go away faster than by talking | The grown-ups would feel better because he was quiet
test: A kid in the playground says, "Sam's dad died and he's playing tag, so he doesn't care, no cap." ("No cap" means "no lie".) What's right? | Wrong; people grieve differently, and Sam may cry at home every night | Right; anyone who plays after a death has stopped caring | Right; grieving people are not allowed to play games | Wrong, because Sam's dad has not really died at all
test: In a film, a girl gets a new puppy the day after her dog dies, and everything is happy again at once. What's off? | A new pet does not replace the old one; many people need time first | Nothing; a new puppy always fixes the grief completely by the next day | Real people never get another pet after one dies | New puppies make people forget the old pet completely
test: Isla's two friends are arguing loudly over whether Grandad is "in heaven" or "just nowhere". What would help Isla more? | Hearing different beliefs kindly and deciding he lives on in her memories | Choosing one friend to win and never speaking to the other | Shouting louder than both of them until they stop | Agreeing that her grandad's life did not matter
test: Aaron's cousin, a bit sarcastic: "yeah, birthday's ruined forever, that's how it works innit." Which is true? | A sad day and a happy day can share a date, and you can hold both | Once someone dies on your birthday you can never enjoy that day again | Birthdays must be moved by law if someone dies that day | Feeling happy on that date would show you never loved her
test: Noah's mate from Boston says, "it's wicked scary thinkin' about everyone dyin' at night." ("Wicked" means very.) What's the best thing for him to do? | Tell a trusted adult the scary thought instead of keeping it alone | Stay up later and later every night until the thought disappears by itself | Watch scary videos about death so that he gets used to it a lot faster | Never tell anyone at all, because the thought means something is wrong
test: Chloe hears kids gossiping that her dad is "doing time". What does that mean, and what helped her? | Being in prison; a teacher quietly checking on her and writing to her dad | Working late; asking her dad to come home earlier every evening | Being in hospital; sending her dad flowers and a card | Being away on a trip; waiting for a postcard from him
test: Kai's coach says, "If a ten-year-old can die in a crash, then kids are never safe." What's wrong with it? | One terrible event feels huge, but most children grow up safe | Nothing, children are never safe anywhere, at any time, in any place | Car crashes only ever happen to children, never adults | The coach is right, so kids should stop playing sport
test: Ella's mum goes, "fixin' to write a thank-you letter to someone who's passed? That's weird, hon." ("Fixin' to" means about to.) What's the reason it isn't weird? | Writing an unsendable letter can still help your feelings | The letter will be delivered to the teacher anyway | Writing letters to someone makes forgetting them completely impossible | It is the law to write to every teacher who dies
test: In a video game, losing a character makes the player sad for five seconds, then he respawns. How is real loss different? | People who die do not come back, and grief can last a long time | Real people respawn too after a short while | Real loss is always over in five seconds as well | Real loss never makes anyone sad at all
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the relationship between grief and the passage of time? | Grief observes no fixed timetable and may be renewed by places and dates | Grief ceases entirely upon the expiry of one calendar month | Grief may be experienced solely upon the day of the bereavement and not thereafter | Grief diminishes only where the bereaved refuses to discuss it
exam: It is given that a child experiences sorrow concerning a relative whom he never met. Determine which statement is consistent with the established facts. | No rule restricts the persons entitled to grieve | Grief is permissible solely in respect of persons well known | Such sorrow constitutes evidence of a disorder of the mind | Grief in respect of unborn relatives is prohibited to children
exam: Ascertain which of the following most accurately describes the position of a child whose parent has been imprisoned. | The parent's wrongdoing is not attributable to the child | The child shares in full the culpability of the parent | The child is obliged to discontinue all affection for the parent | The child shall inevitably commit an identical offence
exam: Select the response consistent with sound reasoning in respect of the question of what follows death. | Beliefs differ among families, and certainty is not available to anyone | The matter has been conclusively determined by the eldest person present | The question may be answered only by those who shout most loudly | The question is improper and must never be raised by a child
